From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: of: Let passive trips follow the ACPI passive
 cooling equation

An ACPI thermal zone describes passive cooling with _TC1, _TC2 and _TSP.
Every _TSP the OS evaluates

  dP = _TC1 * (Tn - Tn-1) + _TC2 * (Tn - Tt)

and lowers performance while dP is positive, so the mitigation keeps
deepening as long as the zone stays above the trip temperature Tt. The
ACPI thermal driver reports the sign of dP as the trend of the trip, and
step_wise turns that into one cooling state per evaluation.

Device tree zones only compare the last two temperature readings.
step_wise then stops deepening the mitigation as soon as the temperature
levels off, wherever that is. Zones modelled on firmware tables that
assume the ACPI equation settle well above their trips: on the Huawei
MateBook E Go, under sustained CPU and GPU load, a board thermistor with a
54 degC passive trip reached 63.7 degC after 20 minutes and was still
rising, while the CPU cooling devices moved between states 1 and 5 (of 20
and 17).

Add three optional properties to passive trips, thermal-constant-1,
thermal-constant-2 and sampling-period-ms, with the meaning of _TC1, _TC2
and _TSP. For a zone with such a trip, thermal_of provides a .get_trend()
that evaluates the equation for that trip once per sampling period, with
the temperature of its previous evaluation as Tn-1, and reports the sign.
Between evaluations it reports a stable trend, so the governor keeps the
current state.

- Below the trip threshold (the trip temperature minus the hysteresis
  once the trip has been crossed) the trend is always dropping. step_wise
  ends the mitigation only on a dropping trend there, and the core stops
  passive polling after that update, so any other answer would leave the
  cooling devices throttled.
- The first evaluation after the zone reaches the trip reports a raising
  trend. dP is not negative there, but at exactly Tt it is 0 and would
  leave the cooling devices at state 0, from which a later dropping trend
  makes step_wise jump to the upper limit of the cooling map.
- A previous sample older than two sampling periods, e.g. across suspend,
  is left out of the _TC1 term.

Trips without these properties, and zones whose sensor driver provides
its own .get_trend(), keep the current behaviour. The per-trip state is
kept by thermal_of, indexed by zone id, rather than in thermal_trip.priv:
that holds the trip node for the cooling map lookup, and
qcom-spmi-temp-alarm reuses it once the zone is registered.

The constants are limited to 65535, and thermal_of warns if passive
polling is missing or slower than the shortest sampling period, since
every evaluation needs a zone update.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 .../bindings/thermal/thermal-zones.yaml       |  44 ++++
 drivers/thermal/thermal_of.c                  | 228 +++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 268 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/thermal-zones.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/thermal-zones.yaml
index 999ad40a20..cadd9281aa 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/thermal-zones.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/thermal-zones.yaml
@@ -179,6 +179,50 @@ patternProperties:
                 The active trip type can be used to control other HW to
                 help in cooling e.g. fans can be sped up or slowed down
 
+            thermal-constant-1:
+              $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+              maximum: 65535
+              description:
+                Weight of the temperature change since the previous evaluation
+                in the passive cooling equation of an ACPI thermal zone (_TC1).
+                Every sampling period, the equation
+                thermal-constant-1 * (Tn - Tn-1) + thermal-constant-2 * (Tn - Tt),
+                with Tt the trip temperature, decides whether the cooling
+                devices of this passive trip go one state deeper (positive) or
+                back (negative), so the mitigation keeps deepening while the
+                zone stays above the trip temperature. Requires
+                thermal-constant-2 and sampling-period-ms.
+
+            thermal-constant-2:
+              $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+              maximum: 65535
+              description:
+                Weight of the distance from the trip temperature in the passive
+                cooling equation (_TC2), see thermal-constant-1.
+
+            sampling-period-ms:
+              minimum: 1
+              description:
+                Time between two evaluations of the passive cooling equation
+                (_TSP), see thermal-constant-1. The zone's
+                polling-delay-passive should be this period or divide it.
+
+          dependentRequired:
+            thermal-constant-1: [ thermal-constant-2, sampling-period-ms ]
+            thermal-constant-2: [ thermal-constant-1, sampling-period-ms ]
+            sampling-period-ms: [ thermal-constant-1, thermal-constant-2 ]
+
+          if:
+            not:
+              properties:
+                type:
+                  const: passive
+          then:
+            properties:
+              thermal-constant-1: false
+              thermal-constant-2: false
+              sampling-period-ms: false
+
           required:
             - temperature
             - hysteresis
diff --git a/drivers/thermal/thermal_of.c b/drivers/thermal/thermal_of.c
index 100fd8a0c8..20df3c2817 100644
--- a/drivers/thermal/thermal_of.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/thermal_of.c
@@ -10,14 +10,50 @@
 
 #include <linux/err.h>
 #include <linux/export.h>
+#include <linux/jiffies.h>
+#include <linux/minmax.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/thermal.h>
+#include <linux/timekeeping.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 #include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/xarray.h>
 
 #include "thermal_core.h"
 
+/* Upper bound of thermal-constant-1/-2, keeps the equation well within s64. */
+#define THERMAL_OF_TC_MAX	U16_MAX
+
+/**
+ * struct thermal_of_trend - passive cooling equation of one trip
+ * @enabled: the trip has valid thermal-constant-1, thermal-constant-2 and
+ *	     sampling-period-ms, see thermal_of_get_trend()
+ * @tc1: weight of the temperature change since the previous evaluation (_TC1)
+ * @tc2: weight of the distance from the trip temperature (_TC2)
+ * @period_ns: time between evaluations (_TSP)
+ * @last_ns: boot time of the previous evaluation, 0 if there is none since the
+ *	     zone last went below the trip threshold
+ * @last_temp: zone temperature at the previous evaluation
+ */
+struct thermal_of_trend {
+	bool enabled;
+	u32 tc1;
+	u32 tc2;
+	u64 period_ns;
+	u64 last_ns;
+	int last_temp;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Arrays of struct thermal_of_trend, one entry per trip in the order of the
+ * zone's trips[], indexed by zone id. Only zones with at least one such trip
+ * have an entry. The data does not hang off thermal_trip.priv: that holds the
+ * trip node for the cooling map lookup, and some sensor drivers reuse it once
+ * the zone is registered.
+ */
+static DEFINE_XARRAY(thermal_of_trends);
+
 /***   functions parsing device tree nodes   ***/
 
 /*
@@ -60,8 +96,38 @@ static int thermal_of_get_trip_type(struct device_node *np,
 	return -ENODEV;
 }
 
+static void thermal_of_trip_trend_init(struct device_node *np,
+				       const struct thermal_trip *trip,
+				       struct thermal_of_trend *tr)
+{
+	u32 period_ms = 0;
+	int found = 0;
+
+	found += !of_property_read_u32(np, "thermal-constant-1", &tr->tc1);
+	found += !of_property_read_u32(np, "thermal-constant-2", &tr->tc2);
+	found += !of_property_read_u32(np, "sampling-period-ms", &period_ms);
+	if (!found)
+		return;
+
+	if (trip->type != THERMAL_TRIP_PASSIVE) {
+		pr_warn("%pOF: passive cooling constants on a %s trip, ignored\n",
+			np, trip_types[trip->type]);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (found != 3 || !period_ms || (!tr->tc1 && !tr->tc2) ||
+	    tr->tc1 > THERMAL_OF_TC_MAX || tr->tc2 > THERMAL_OF_TC_MAX) {
+		pr_warn("%pOF: invalid passive cooling constants, ignored\n", np);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	tr->period_ns = (u64)period_ms * NSEC_PER_MSEC;
+	tr->enabled = true;
+}
+
 static int thermal_of_populate_trip(struct device_node *np,
-				    struct thermal_trip *trip)
+				    struct thermal_trip *trip,
+				    struct thermal_of_trend *tr)
 {
 	int prop;
 	int ret;
@@ -90,14 +156,19 @@ static int thermal_of_populate_trip(struct device_node *np,
 
 	trip->priv = np;
 
+	if (tr)
+		thermal_of_trip_trend_init(np, trip, tr);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static struct thermal_trip *thermal_of_trips_init(struct device_node *np, int *ntrips)
+static struct thermal_trip *thermal_of_trips_init(struct device_node *np, int *ntrips,
+						  struct thermal_of_trend **trends)
 {
 	int ret, count;
 
 	*ntrips = 0;
+	*trends = NULL;
 
 	struct device_node *trips __free(device_node) = of_get_child_by_name(np, "trips");
 	if (!trips)
@@ -111,18 +182,130 @@ static struct thermal_trip *thermal_of_trips_init(struct device_node *np, int *n
 	if (!tt)
 		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
 
+	/* Without memory for this, the zone keeps the core's trend. */
+	struct thermal_of_trend *tr __free(kfree) = kzalloc_objs(*tr, count);
+
 	count = 0;
 	for_each_child_of_node_scoped(trips, trip) {
-		ret = thermal_of_populate_trip(trip, &tt[count++]);
+		ret = thermal_of_populate_trip(trip, &tt[count], tr ? &tr[count] : NULL);
 		if (ret)
 			return ERR_PTR(ret);
+		count++;
 	}
 
 	*ntrips = count;
+	*trends = no_free_ptr(tr);
 
 	return no_free_ptr(tt);
 }
 
+/*
+ * An ACPI thermal zone describes passive cooling with _TC1, _TC2 and _TSP.
+ * Every _TSP the OS evaluates
+ *
+ *   dP = _TC1 * (Tn - Tn-1) + _TC2 * (Tn - Tt)
+ *
+ * and lowers performance while dP is positive, so the mitigation keeps
+ * deepening as long as the zone stays above the trip temperature Tt. The
+ * comparison of the last two readings that is used otherwise stops deepening
+ * as soon as the temperature levels off, wherever that is.
+ *
+ * Like the ACPI thermal driver, report the sign of dP as the trend of the trip.
+ * step_wise turns that into one cooling state per evaluation.
+ */
+static int thermal_of_get_trend(struct thermal_zone_device *tz,
+				const struct thermal_trip *trip,
+				enum thermal_trend *trend)
+{
+	const struct thermal_trip_desc *td = trip_to_trip_desc(trip);
+	struct thermal_of_trend *trends, *tr;
+	int temp = tz->temperature;
+	u64 now, elapsed, slack;
+	s64 dp;
+
+	trends = xa_load(&thermal_of_trends, tz->id);
+	if (!trends)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	tr = &trends[thermal_zone_trip_id(tz, trip)];
+	if (!tr->enabled)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * Below the threshold step_wise ends the mitigation only on a dropping
+	 * trend, and the core stops passive polling after this update. Any other
+	 * answer would leave the cooling devices throttled.
+	 */
+	if (temp < td->threshold) {
+		tr->last_ns = 0;
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_DROPPING;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	now = ktime_get_boottime_ns();
+	elapsed = now - tr->last_ns;
+
+	/*
+	 * The zone has just reached the trip temperature: start the mitigation.
+	 * dP is not negative here, but at exactly Tt it is 0, which would leave
+	 * the cooling devices at state 0. A dropping trend in that state makes
+	 * step_wise jump to the upper limit of the cooling map.
+	 */
+	if (!tr->last_ns) {
+		tr->last_ns = now;
+		tr->last_temp = temp;
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_RAISING;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Keep the current state until the next evaluation is due. The core
+	 * rounds polling delays longer than a second to whole seconds, which
+	 * can bring a poll up to a quarter second early. Short periods still
+	 * need at least half a period between evaluations.
+	 */
+	slack = min_t(u64, max_t(u64, tr->period_ns / 8, jiffies_to_nsecs(HZ / 4)),
+		      tr->period_ns / 2);
+	if (elapsed + slack < tr->period_ns) {
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_STABLE;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	dp = (s64)tr->tc2 * ((s64)temp - trip->temperature);
+	/* Leave out the _TC1 term if the previous sample is stale, e.g. after suspend. */
+	if (elapsed <= 2 * tr->period_ns)
+		dp += (s64)tr->tc1 * ((s64)temp - tr->last_temp);
+
+	tr->last_ns = now;
+	tr->last_temp = temp;
+
+	if (dp > 0)
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_RAISING;
+	else if (dp < 0)
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_DROPPING;
+	else
+		*trend = THERMAL_TREND_STABLE;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Decide whether the zone uses the passive cooling equation. Returns the
+ * shortest sampling period in ms, or 0 if no trip has valid constants.
+ */
+static u32 thermal_of_trend_period(const struct thermal_of_trend *trends, int ntrips)
+{
+	u64 min_ns = 0;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ntrips; i++) {
+		if (trends[i].enabled && (!min_ns || trends[i].period_ns < min_ns))
+			min_ns = trends[i].period_ns;
+	}
+
+	return div_u64(min_ns, NSEC_PER_MSEC);
+}
+
 static struct device_node *of_thermal_zone_find(struct device_node *sensor, int id)
 {
 	struct of_phandle_args sensor_specs;
@@ -358,8 +541,12 @@ static bool thermal_of_should_bind(struct thermal_zone_device *tz,
  */
 static void thermal_of_zone_unregister(struct thermal_zone_device *tz)
 {
+	/* A zone update that already holds the data finishes before unregistering. */
+	struct thermal_of_trend *trends = xa_erase(&thermal_of_trends, tz->id);
+
 	thermal_zone_device_disable(tz);
 	thermal_zone_device_unregister(tz);
+	kfree(trends);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -388,10 +575,12 @@ static struct thermal_zone_device *thermal_of_zone_register(struct device_node *
 	struct thermal_zone_device_ops of_ops = *ops;
 	struct thermal_zone_device *tz;
 	struct thermal_trip *trips;
+	struct thermal_of_trend *trends;
 	struct thermal_zone_params tzp = {};
 	struct device_node *np;
 	const char *action;
 	int delay, pdelay;
+	u32 trend_period;
 	int ntrips;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -402,7 +591,7 @@ static struct thermal_zone_device *thermal_of_zone_register(struct device_node *
 		return ERR_CAST(np);
 	}
 
-	trips = thermal_of_trips_init(np, &ntrips);
+	trips = thermal_of_trips_init(np, &ntrips, &trends);
 	if (IS_ERR(trips)) {
 		pr_err("Failed to parse trip points for %pOFP id=%d\n", sensor, id);
 		ret = PTR_ERR(trips);
@@ -422,6 +611,23 @@ static struct thermal_zone_device *thermal_of_zone_register(struct device_node *
 
 	of_ops.should_bind = thermal_of_should_bind;
 
+	trend_period = trends ? thermal_of_trend_period(trends, ntrips) : 0;
+	if (trend_period && of_ops.get_trend) {
+		pr_warn("%pOFP: the sensor provides the trend, passive cooling constants ignored\n",
+			np);
+		trend_period = 0;
+	} else if (trend_period) {
+		of_ops.get_trend = thermal_of_get_trend;
+		/* Every evaluation needs a zone update while the trip is reached. */
+		if (!(pdelay ?: delay) || (pdelay ?: delay) > trend_period)
+			pr_warn("%pOFP: passive polling should not be slower than the sampling period of %u ms\n",
+				np, trend_period);
+	}
+	if (!trend_period) {
+		kfree(trends);
+		trends = NULL;
+	}
+
 	ret = of_property_read_string(np, "critical-action", &action);
 	if (!ret && !of_ops.critical) {
 		if (!strcasecmp(action, "reboot"))
@@ -439,6 +645,19 @@ static struct thermal_zone_device *thermal_of_zone_register(struct device_node *
 		goto out_kfree_trips;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The zone is still disabled, so .get_trend() normally runs only after
+	 * this. Without the data it reports an error and the core falls back to
+	 * its own trend.
+	 */
+	if (trends) {
+		ret = xa_err(xa_store(&thermal_of_trends, tz->id, trends, GFP_KERNEL));
+		if (ret) {
+			pr_warn("%pOFP: passive cooling constants not applied: %d\n", np, ret);
+			kfree(trends);
+		}
+	}
+
 	of_node_put(np);
 	kfree(trips);
 
@@ -454,6 +673,7 @@ static struct thermal_zone_device *thermal_of_zone_register(struct device_node *
 
 out_kfree_trips:
 	kfree(trips);
+	kfree(trends);
 out_of_node_put:
 	of_node_put(np);
 
